7901: that external Lean or BEDC semantics were rebuilt, or that theorem-like material
7902: outside $\mathcal E$ is covered.
7903: \end{theorem}
7904: 
7905: \begin{proof}
7906: The extractor is a finite program whose inputs are exactly the two byte strings
7907: at \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  It scans the source lines
7908: for beginnings of the five displayed environment names, records the first
7909: \path{\textbackslash label\{...\}} before the matching end of the same
7910: environment, and forms the finite set $T$ of recorded labels together with line
7911: numbers, titles, and environment names.  It also reads the inventory JSON and
7912: forms the finite set $I$ of labels appearing in inventory label fields with one
7913: of the package theorem-label prefixes.  These operations are deterministic
7914: functions of the two input byte strings.
7915: 
7916: The script exits with code \path{0} exactly when three finite tests pass: there
7917: is no element of $\mathcal E$ without a label, no duplicate label in $T$, and
7918: $T\subseteq I$.  Therefore a refreshed exit-code-zero log proves precisely the
7919: syntactic coverage assertion: every labelled theorem-like environment in the
7920: chosen domain has a corresponding inventory label and the source labels are
7921: well-formed for this comparison.  If any test fails, the printed missing-label,
7922: duplicate-label, or omitted-from-inventory list is the finite bad subrecord.
7923: 
7924: No step of this computation parses mathematical statements, checks proofs,
7925: invokes Lean, consults the publication daemon, or validates external artifacts.
7926: Thus the certificate is stronger than the earlier manual ledger at the single
7927: point needed by the scope contract---deterministic environment-label coverage---
7928: and no stronger than that.  The semantic and implementation boundaries remain
7929: those of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and
7930: Corollary~\ref{cor:finite-record-gate-soundness-boundary}.
7931: \end{proof}
7932: 
7933: \begin{theorem}[Extractor-domain exactness and non-gluing]
7934: \label{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing}
7935: For the submitted theorem-inventory surface, the command
7936: \path{python review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py} is exact for one
7937: domain and impossible to glue beyond that domain without an added finite
7938: coordinate.  Its exact domain is the syntactic label-coverage predicate on
7939: \[
7940: \mathcal E=\{\texttt{definition},\texttt{lemma},\texttt{proposition},
7941: \texttt{theorem},\texttt{corollary}\}.
7942: \]
7943: An exit-code-zero run proves precisely that the source has no unlabelled
7944: environment in $\mathcal E$, no duplicate label among those environments, and no
7945: labelled environment in $\mathcal E$ omitted from \path{theorem_inventory.json}.
7946: It proves no audit-record coverage, statement-semantics coverage,
7947: proof-correctness coverage, Lean/BEDC rebuild, artifact validation, daemon run,
7948: or venue-state fact.  Any manuscript reading that adds one of those conclusions
7949: is a properly stronger reading of the theorem-inventory coordinate and is
7950: accepted only after the submitted surface adds the corresponding parser domain,
7951: semantic checker, proof checker, build log, artifact log, daemon log, or venue
7952: record with assigned pass rows.
7953: \end{theorem}
7954: 
7955: \begin{proof}
7956: The script definition fixes the domain: its \path{THEOREM_ENVS} tuple contains
7957: exactly the five displayed names, and its begin-pattern is built from that tuple.
7958: The extraction loop records only occurrences of those begins in \path{main.tex},
7959: then searches within the matched environment for the first \path{\label{...}}
7960: token.  The inventory side reads only label strings from
7961: \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  Thus the finite predicate computed by the script
7962: has exactly three positive clauses: every detected environment in $\mathcal E$
7963: has a label, the detected labels are not duplicated, and the detected label set
7964: is contained in the inventory label set.  Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}
7965: therefore gives exactness on this syntactic domain.
7966: 
7967: Each proposed extension reads a coordinate absent from that computation.  Audit
7968: records use a different LaTeX environment counter and are not in $\mathcal E$;
7969: statement semantics would require a parser and meaning relation for theorem
7970: bodies; proof correctness would require a proof checker or a formal build;
7971: Lean/BEDC rebuilding, artifact validation, daemon execution, and venue state each
7972: require their own command, source state, environment, exit code, and log or
7973: external record.  Two package states can agree on \path{main.tex} labels in
7974: $\mathcal E$ and on \path{theorem_inventory.json} while differing in any of
7975: those absent coordinates.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary},
7976: the extractor's accepted finite record cannot determine such a stronger fact.
7977: 
7978: Finally, in the stronger-reading preorder of
7979: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}, adding any of the excluded
7980: conclusions enlarges the claim-kind, evidence-surface, command-boundary, or
7981: assigned-gate coordinate.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}
7982: then supplies the positive direction: once the enlarged parser domain, semantic
7983: checker, proof checker, build log, artifact log, daemon log, or venue record and
7984: its pass rows are included, that stronger coordinate may be checked by the
7985: finite interface.  Without those additions,
7986: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the missing
7987: coordinate as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the extractor is exact and maximal
7988: for syntactic label coverage over $\mathcal E$, and it cannot be glued into any
7989: semantic, auditrecord, implementation, artifact, daemon, or venue certificate by
7990: prose alone.
7991: \end{proof}
7992: 
7993: 
7994: \begin{theorem}[Final-byte extractor inventory inclusion]
7995: \label{thm:final-byte-extractor-inventory-inclusion}
7996: Fix a manuscript source byte string $M$, an inventory byte string $J$, and the
7997: extractor byte string
7998: \path{review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py}.  Let $T(M)$ be the finite
7999: set of labels obtained by running that extractor on the five-environment domain
8000: $\mathcal E$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary},
8001: and let $I(J)$ be the finite set of inventory labels read from $J$ with the
8002: accepted theorem-label prefixes.  For the final bytes of this package, a
8003: zero-exit extractor run is equivalent to the following three finite conditions:
8004: \[
8005:   T(M)\subseteq I(J),
8006:   \qquad \text{no element of }\mathcal E\text{ in }M\text{ is unlabelled},
8007:   \qquad \text{and }T(M)\text{ has no duplicate source label}.
8008: \]
8009: Consequently, after any edit to \path{main.tex} or \path{theorem_inventory.json},
8010: the exact theorem-obligation ledger coordinate used by
8011: Theorems~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} and
8012: \ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} is available only from a fresh
8013: zero-exit run on those final bytes.  This inclusion criterion is the complete
8014: bad-example obstruction for missing in-domain labels: an omitted labelled
8015: environment, an unlabelled in-domain environment, or a duplicate in-domain label
8016: is witnessed by a finite failing row printed by the extractor, and no prose
8017: inventory statement can remove that row without changing $M$, $J$, or the
8018: extractor coordinate.
8019: \end{theorem}
8020: 
8021: \begin{proof}
8022: The first two input byte strings determine the two finite sets.  The extractor
8023: constructs $T(M)$ by a line scan for begins of the five names in $\mathcal E$,
8024: searches each matched environment body for its first label token, and records the
8025: label together with its line and title fields.  It constructs $I(J)$ by reading
8026: the inventory JSON and collecting the finite label fields with the accepted
8027: prefixes.  Since these operations use only fixed regular expressions and JSON
8028: parsing on finite byte strings, $T(M)$, the unlabelled-environment list, the
8029: duplicate-label list, and $I(J)$ are deterministic functions of the final bytes.
8030: 
8031: The extractor's acceptance test is exactly the conjunction of the three displayed
8032: conditions.  If an in-domain environment has no label, the unlabelled list is
8033: nonempty and the command exits nonzero.  If two in-domain environments have the
8034: same label, the duplicate list is nonempty and the command exits nonzero.  If a
8035: recorded source label is absent from the inventory, then the set difference
8036: $T(M)\setminus I(J)$ is nonempty and the command exits nonzero.  Conversely, when
8037: all three lists are empty and $T(M)\subseteq I(J)$, the extractor has no failing
8038: row for its declared predicate and exits with code zero.  This proves the stated
8039: equivalence.
8040: 
8041: It remains to connect the equivalence to the paper's support-coordinate chain.
8042: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} uses the inventory only as a
8043: finite theorem-obligation ledger plus the syntactic extractor coordinate, and
8044: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} requires that the inventory,
8045: extractor run, verifier logs, and final digest be ordered after the final source
8046: bytes are selected.  Editing either $M$ or $J$ changes one of the deterministic
8047: inputs just described.  The exact ledger coordinate for the edited package
8048: therefore requires the fresh zero-exit run on those edited bytes; otherwise the
8049: package has at most the weaker path-verified or stale-coordinate reading already
8050: classified in Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-support-coordinate-dichotomy}.
8051: 
8052: The bad-example statement follows from the same finite disjunction.  Each failure
8053: mode is represented by an explicit finite row: an unlabelled environment, a
8054: duplicate label, or a member of $T(M)\setminus I(J)$.  Because
8055: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} allows the submitted
8056: claim to depend only on recorded finite coordinates, prose cannot erase such a
8057: row.  Only changing the source bytes, the inventory bytes, or the extractor
8058: coordinate can change the computed obstruction.  Thus the theorem gives the
8059: final-byte inclusion gate required for the post-inventory theorem chain and no
8060: semantic proof-checking, Lean/BEDC rebuild, artifact, daemon, upload, or venue
8061: claim beyond that syntactic gate.
8062: \end{proof}
8063: 
8064: \begin{theorem}[Theorem-inventory proof-checker obstruction]
8065: \label{thm:theorem-inventory-proofchecker-obstruction}
8066: Let $\mathcal E$ be the five-environment domain
8067: \{\path{definition}, \path{lemma}, \path{proposition}, \path{theorem},
8068: \path{corollary}\} in \path{main.tex}.  The finite theorem-inventory support
8069: surface consisting of \path{theorem_inventory.json}, \path{theorem_inventory.md},
8070: and the output of \path{python review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py}
8071: classifies exactly labelled-environment coverage in $\mathcal E$: every labelled
8072: environment in $\mathcal E$ is present in the inventory, no such label is missing
8073: or duplicated, and no inventory conclusion glues to material outside that domain.
8074: It is not a proof checker.  Any stronger reading that validates proof semantics,
8075: checks theorem statements against Lean or BEDC, covers other TeX environments,
8076: covers \path{submission_abstract.tex}, validates remarks or audit records, or
8077: certifies artifact or venue facts is accepted by this calculus if and only if the
8078: support surface is extended by a nonempty finite checker coordinate for that
8079: additional domain and by an assigned pass row.
8080: \end{theorem}
8081: 
8082: \begin{proof}
8083: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary} identifies
8084: the extractor domain as the five classes in $\mathcal E$ and records that the
8085: script reads \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  Theorem~\ref{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing}
8086: then proves non-gluing: the successful extractor run eliminates exactly the bad
8087: example of a labelled environment in $\mathcal E$ missing from the inventory or a
8088: duplicate label in that same syntactic domain, and it says nothing about other
8089: files, environments, proof semantics, or external artifacts.
8090: 
8091: To see that no proof-checking conclusion follows, fix two TeX states with the
8092: same sequence of labelled $\mathcal E$ environments and the same inventory rows,
8093: but with different proof bodies for one theorem.  For example, replace a proof
8094: body by another body while leaving the surrounding \path{\textbackslash begin}
8095: line, label, environment kind, and inventory row unchanged.  The extractor reads
8096: only the syntactic environment record and the inventory labels, so it returns the
8097: same coverage result on both states.  A semantic proof checker could distinguish
8098: the states, but that checker output is not part of the finite projection.  By
8099: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a semantic theorem
8100: validation claim is therefore not determined by the inventory support surface.
8101: 
8102: The same argument applies to every listed stronger domain: each requires reading
8103: at least one new file, environment class, proof-assistant state, artifact log, or
8104: venue record that is absent from the extractor projection.  Hence each stronger
8105: reading has a finite bad subrecord unless a new checker coordinate and assigned
8106: pass row are submitted.  Conversely, once that coordinate is submitted, the claim
8107: is no longer a gluing inference from the old inventory; it is a separate finite
8108: extension governed by Theorem~\ref{thm:record-interface-rigidity}.  This proves
8109: both exact syntactic coverage and the proof-checker obstruction.
8110: \end{proof}
8111: 
8112: \begin{theorem}[Theorem-inventory boundary]
8113: \label{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}
8114: This theorem retains the legacy cross-reference label
8115: \path{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} for compatibility with earlier
8116: acceptance-ledger entries; its environment category is \path{theorem}, not
8117: \path{proposition}.
8118: The file \path{theorem_inventory.json} is a theorem-obligation inventory record whose
8119: current support has two finite parts.  First, it is an obligation ledger selected
8120: by finite-record review and connected to the manifest and acceptance ledger.  Second,
8121: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary} supplies a
8122: deterministic syntactic comparison between the labelled theorem-like environments
8123: in the five-environment domain $\mathcal E$ and the labels appearing in the
8124: inventory.  The inventory indexes obligations; the evidence for those obligations
8125: is supplied by the corresponding TeX statements and proofs in this source,
8126: together with external records only where a statement explicitly cites them.  It
8127: is not a semantic proof checker and it is not an executable completeness
8128: certificate for theorem-like material outside $\mathcal E$.  Consequently any
8129: claim that the ledger itself semantically checks the printed proofs, reruns Lean
8130: or BEDC, validates artifacts, or certifies an external venue state is rejected by
8131: the current finite-record interface, while the bounded obligation-ledger and
8132: syntactic environment-label coverage claims are accepted.
8133: \end{theorem}
8134: 
8135: \begin{proof}
8136: The support records named in Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger}
8137: are finite manifest and ledger records.  The root manifest exposes the path
8138: \path{theorem_inventory.json}; acceptance-ledger entry \path{a7} records the
8139: inventory, marker-existence, theorem-environment extraction, and submission-pack
8140: gates for the bounded theorem-ledger claim; and the certificate verifier checks
8141: only record shape, gate coverage, evidence pointers, and file availability.  The
8142: new extractor coordinates add a command, source state, environment, exit code,
8143: and log path for syntactic label coverage in the domain $\mathcal E$.  By
8144: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}, those
8145: coordinates prove only that all labelled environments in the chosen domain are
8146: represented by labels in the inventory and that no source label is missing or
8147: duplicated.
8148: 
8149: The remaining boundary is semantic, not syntactic.  A finite inventory can still
8150: be useful because it fixes the Stage-A obligation surface that the submission
8151: chooses to expose and lets the certificate ledger prevent hidden prose-only
8152: references to that surface.  The extractor strengthens this by eliminating one
8153: bad example: an environment in $\mathcal E$ whose label exists in \path{main.tex}
8154: but is absent from the inventory.  Once the extractor log has exit code
8155: \path{0}, that bad example cannot occur.  Finite-coordinate determination is
8156: still exact, however.  The accepted records are manifest, ledger, table, and
8157: syntactic extractor coordinates; they cannot imply a nonrecorded semantic
8158: theorem checker, nonrecorded Lean rebuild, nonrecorded daemon run, or nonrecorded
8159: artifact validation.  Hence the finite interface accepts the obligation ledger
8160: together with deterministic syntactic environment-label coverage and rejects
8161: every stronger proof-checking or implementation-soundness reading.
8162: \end{proof}
8163: 
8164: \begin{proposition}[Regenerated theorem-inventory digest closure]
8165: \label{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure}
8166: Suppose a theorem-inventory pass regenerates either \path{theorem_inventory.json} or
8167: \path{theorem_inventory.md} after the package has selected the exact-inclusion
8168: support branch.  Then the final support-surface claims of
8169: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, the submitted theorem-ledger
8170: claim of Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}, and the command
8171: boundary of Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory} remain closed
8172: only under the fixed-point conditions of
8173: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}: the package refreshes the
8174: corresponding SHA-256 rows in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
8175: reruns the finite record verifiers and the theorem-environment extractor whose
8176: records name that digest manifest or inventory coordinate, and does not promote
8177: primary-claim or venue-compliance rows beyond the fresh records actually
8178: included.  Once those rows and logs are refreshed, the regeneration creates no
8179: new mathematical, Lean, daemon, or artifact claim: it only changes the
8180: byte-coordinate of the theorem-obligation ledger and its syntactic label-coverage
8181: certificate inside the already selected finite support surface.
8182: \end{proposition}
8183: 
8184: \begin{proof}
8185: The selected support branch is byte-level exact inclusion.  In that branch,
8186: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} identifies the submitted support
8187: surface by relative paths together with the digest rows in
8188: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The theorem inventory is one of
8189: those paths: Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger} exposes
8190: \path{theorem_inventory.json} through the root manifest and acceptance-ledger
8191: entry \path{a7}, while the final digest record also lists the human-readable
8192: companion \path{theorem_inventory.md}.  Therefore regenerating either file after
8193: the digest record was written changes a finite coordinate used by the exact
8194: support-surface relation.  Until the SHA-256 rows are recomputed, the digest
8195: record denotes the old bytes, and the regenerated inventory is not the same
8196: finite certificate file as the one named by the final packaging record.
8197: 
8198: The required reruns are exactly the finite record verifiers and extractor at the
8199: package boundary.  The certificate-record verifier checks that the
8200: manifest-required paths, including the theorem-inventory coordinate, are present
8201: and covered by the accepted gate records; the source-interface and primary-claim
8202: verifiers record their command boundary against the same final digest manifest;
8203: and the theorem-environment extractor compares labelled environments in
8204: $\mathcal E$ with the inventory labels.  Rerunning those commands after the
8205: digest refresh supplies fresh source state, environment, exit code, and log-path
8206: coordinates for the command-boundary table.  No verifier in this list proves
8207: theorem semantics.  Hence the refreshed state is closed precisely as a finite
8208: record-and-digest package with deterministic syntactic label coverage, and by
8209: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} it remains a
8210: non-semantic theorem-obligation ledger rather than a proof-checking certificate.
8211: \end{proof}
8212: 
8213: \begin{corollary}[Current theorem-inventory digest instance]
8214: \label{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance}
8215: For the present local support package, after \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
8216: \path{theorem_inventory.md} have been regenerated, the submitted
8217: theorem-obligation ledger may be cited in
8218: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} only through the refreshed
8219: digest rows for those two files in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}
8220: and through fresh finite-record verifier logs produced after the regenerated
8221: inventory files are present.  If either the two digest rows or the
8222: post-regeneration verifier logs are absent, the
8223: inventory remains a local index but is not closed as an exact-inclusion
8224: support coordinate for the bundle-backed theorem-ledger claim.
8225: \end{corollary}
8226: 
8227: \begin{proof}
8228: Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} to the
8229: two regenerated inventory files in this package.  The support branch selected by
8230: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} is exact inclusion, so the
8231: identity of each submitted support file is read from the corresponding
8232: relative-path and SHA-256 row in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.
8233: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} uses
8234: \path{theorem_inventory.json} as the theorem-ledger coordinate and uses
8235: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary} only for
8236: syntactic label coverage.  Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}
8237: excludes every stronger semantic-proof reading.  Therefore the only new fact
8238: needed after regeneration is byte-coordinate and command-coordinate closure: the
8239: digest rows must name the new bytes, and the finite verifiers and extractor whose
8240: records mention the digest or inventory coordinate must be rerun against the
8241: package state in which the regenerated inventory files exist.  Without those rows
8242: and logs, the ledger is still a local file, but the exact-inclusion support
8243: relation points either to stale bytes or to an unverifiable command-boundary
8244: state.  With them, no additional mathematical, Lean, daemon, or artifact claim is
8245: introduced; the refreshed package supplies exactly the finite support coordinate
8246: used by the submitted theorem-obligation ledger.
8247: \end{proof}
8248: 
8249: \begin{proposition}[Post-inventory fixed-point support certificate]
8250: \label{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate}
8251: In the present package state, the two regenerated theorem-inventory files are
8252: accepted by the exact-inclusion theorem-ledger chain only at the following
8253: finite fixed point: the rows of
8254: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} name
8255: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}; the same digest
8256: manifest names the refreshed logs
8257: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
8258: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log},
8259: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log}, and
8260: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log}; and
8261: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} records those four invocations as
8262: finite checks with exit code \path{0}.  Under these hypotheses,
8263: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance} applies to the
8264: theorem-ledger coordinate used by
8265: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  If any one of the two
8266: inventory digest rows or four refreshed command-log rows is replaced by a
8267: pre-regeneration row, the exact-inclusion support claim fails and only the local
8268: historical index remains.
8269: \end{proposition}
8270: 
8271: \begin{proof}
8272: The statement is a finite-coordinate instance of
8273: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}.  The first named coordinate
8274: is \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and the second is
8275: \path{theorem_inventory.md}; after regeneration, each coordinate is identified
8276: inside the exact-inclusion surface only by the SHA-256 row attached to its
8277: current byte string in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  The four
8278: command-run coordinates are exactly the finite verifier and extractor logs listed
8279: in Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}: the certificate-record verifier,
8280: the source-interface record verifier, the primary-claim inventory verifier, and
8281: the theorem-environment extractor.  The same table records command, source state,
8282: environment, exit code, and log path, and its boundary column states that these
8283: are record or syntactic label-coverage checks rather than Lean, daemon, Rule110,
8284: upload, or semantic validation runs.
8285: 
8286: If the five rows are current, the hypotheses of
8287: Proposition~\ref{prop:regenerated-theorem-inventory-digest-closure} are met for
8288: the regenerated inventory files and for the verifier and extractor records whose
8289: logs name the digest or inventory boundary.
8290: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance} then supplies
8291: exactly the local fixed-point instance needed by the theorem-obligation ledger in
8292: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  Conversely, suppose that
8293: one of the two inventory digest rows is stale.  Then the digest manifest denotes
8294: old bytes at one of the two inventory paths, so the regenerated file is not the
8295: file certified by the exact-inclusion row.  Suppose instead that one of the
8296: four command-log rows is stale.  Then the command-boundary table promotes a
8297: fresh verifier invocation while the digest surface still names an earlier log
8298: file, so the command-run coordinate is not closed in the selected byte surface.
8299: In either case the bad subrecord is finite: it is the stale row together with
8300: its relative path.  The exact-inclusion claim therefore fails by
8301: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, while
8302: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} still permits the
8303: weaker reading of the inventory as a local historical manual index.
8304: \end{proof}
8305: 
8306: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record post-inventory realization invariant]
8307: \label{thm:post-inventory-finite-record-realization}
8308: Suppose the following finite records are present in the current source package:
8309: \begin{enumerate}
8310: \item \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md};
8311: \item a zero-exit log for
8312: \path{python review_bundle/extract_theorem_environments.py} computed after those
8313: two files were written;
8314: \item zero-exit logs for
8315: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py},
8316: \path{python review_bundle/verify_source_interface_record.py}, and
8317: \path{python review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py} computed against the
8318: same post-inventory package state; and
8319: \item a regenerated \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} whose rows name
8320: the two inventory files and the four log files just listed.
8321: \end{enumerate}
8322: Then the first coordinate in
8323: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} is instantiated as
8324: a local exact-inclusion theorem-ledger support coordinate.  The instantiated
8325: coordinate is exactly syntactic inventory coverage plus finite-record verifier
8326: closure: it is not a semantic proof check, a Lean rebuild, a daemon run, an
8327: artifact rerun, a venue upload receipt, or an archive-equivalent locator.  If one
8328: of the four listed conditions is absent, the theorem-inventory files remain at
8329: most local historical indices and cannot support the exact-inclusion
8330: theorem-ledger claim.
8331: \end{theorem}
8332: 
8333: \begin{proof}
8334: The proof is by finite-coordinate determination.  The first condition supplies
8335: the two byte strings that are to be accepted as the theorem-ledger inventory.
8336: The second condition supplies the only syntactic coverage test used by
8337: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}: the
8338: extractor reads \path{main.tex}, extracts theorem-like labels, and compares them
8339: with labels present in \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  The third condition
8340: supplies the companion finite-record checks for the certificate ledger, the
8341: source-interface record, and the primary-claim inventory.  The fourth condition
8342: places the resulting bytes and logs into the selected exact-inclusion digest
8343: surface.
8344: 
8345: Under all four conditions, every finite coordinate named in
8346: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate} is present:
8347: the inventory-file coordinates are identified by SHA-256 rows, and the extractor
8348: and verifier coordinates are identified by their zero-exit command logs and
8349: digest rows.  Therefore
8350: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-theorem-inventory-digest-instance} applies, giving the
8351: local exact-inclusion support coordinate used in the first clause of
8352: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.  The boundary of
8353: the coordinate is the intersection of the boundaries of its inputs: the
8354: extractor is syntactic label coverage only, the three verifiers are finite-record
8355: checks only, and the digest manifest is a byte-coordinate manifest only.  None of
8356: these records contains a proof-assistant rebuild, publication-daemon run,
8357: artifact validation rerun, external upload receipt, or archive locator, so no
8358: such stronger coordinate can be inferred.
8359: 
8360: Conversely, omit any one condition.  If the inventory files are absent, there is
8361: no byte string for the theorem-ledger row.  If the extractor log is absent or
8362: pre-inventory, syntactic coverage is not recorded for the current inventory
8363: bytes.  If any finite-record verifier log is absent or pre-inventory, the
8364: corresponding support record is not closed against the same package state.  If
8365: the digest manifest is not regenerated over the inventory files and logs, the
8366: exact-inclusion surface names stale or missing byte coordinates.  In each case
8367: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} rejects the stronger
8368: reading and leaves only whatever local historical index was actually recorded.
8369: \end{proof}
8370: 
8371: \begin{theorem}[Seven-row submitted theorem-obligation ledger integration]
8372: \label{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}
8373: Let $P^{+}_{7}$ be the seven-row support state obtained from $P^{+}_{6}$
8374: by the conservative finite-record extension of
8375: Theorem~\ref{thm:nested-package-states}, namely by adding exactly the submitted theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}.  In
8376: $P^{+}_{7}$, the theorem-obligation ledger is no longer a detached review-only
8377: artifact: the submitted finite support surface contains
8378: \path{theorem_inventory.json} through the manifest field \path{theorem_inventory}
8379: and through acceptance-ledger entry \path{a7}.  The earlier six-row state
8380: $P^{+}_{6}$ of Theorems~\ref{thm:nested-package-states}
8381: and~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} remains
8382: the row-frozen certificate state for \path{a1}--\path{a6}; the present theorem
8383: adds exactly the theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}.  Consequently the
8384: package may make
8385: the following bounded theorem-ledger claim: every theorem-obligation assertion
8386: made by the submission is supported only through the finite coordinates in
8387: Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger}, namely the integrated
8388: theorem inventory, the primary-claim inventory, the certificate ledger, and the
8389: manifest exposure records.  By
8390: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} and
8391: Theorem~\ref{thm:manual-ledger-dependence-boundary}, this is a theorem-obligation ledger claim with deterministic syntactic
8392: theorem-environment label coverage, not a semantic proof-checking or
8393: implementation-soundness claim.  The claim does not assert that
8394: \path{theorem_inventory.json} proves the mathematical semantics of each theorem,
8395: does not replace the proofs printed in this source, and does not certify a Lean
8396: rebuild, publication-daemon run, Rule110 artifact rerun, semantic extractor
8397: completeness beyond $\mathcal E$, or external upload.
8398: \end{theorem}
8399: 
8400: \begin{proof}
8401: The support surface is finite and can be inspected coordinate by coordinate.
8402: The root manifest now contains the key \path{theorem_inventory} with value
8403: \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  The certificate schema includes the same key in
8404: its required manifest-file map.  Therefore a certificate-record verification of
8405: the enlarged package must check that the manifest exposes this root-level
8406: inventory path and that the file is present.  This removes the previous
8407: possibility of silently citing a root-level theorem-inventory artifact outside the support
8408: surface.
8409: 
8410: The acceptance ledger contains the additional entry \path{a7}.  Its accepted
8411: claim is exactly the bounded submitted theorem-obligation ledger claim; its
8412: claim kinds are \path{traceability} and \path{submission}.  Under the schema's
8413: gate dispatch, those kinds require the inventory, marker-existence, and
8414: submission-pack gates; the theorem-extractor check is an additional command
8415: boundary attached to the marker-existence gate.  Entry \path{a7} supplies the
8416: required pass records: the inventory gate points to \path{theorem_inventory.json},
8417: the marker-existence gate points to
8418: Table~\ref{tab:submitted-theorem-obligation-ledger} and the extractor log, and
8419: the submission-pack gate points to \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}.  Thus the
8420: ledger supplies a finite route from the theorem-inventory file to the submitted
8421: certificate records rather than a prose-only reference.
8422: 
8423: It remains to identify the boundary.  The schema-level verifier checks finite
8424: record shape, required manifest paths, gate coverage, evidence pointers, and
8425: evidence levels.  None of those checks is a semantic proof of the theorem-like
8426: environments in this source, and none reruns Lean, the publication daemon, or the
8427: Rule110/GroundCompiler artifacts.  The independent theorem-environment extractor
8428: only supplies the syntactic coverage certificate of
8429: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}.
8430: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary} and
8431: Theorem~\ref{thm:manual-ledger-dependence-boundary} supply the remaining
8432: classification: the inventory is an accepted Stage-A ledger with syntactic label
8433: coverage and not an executable semantic completeness certificate.  The theorem-ledger claim is
8434: therefore admissible only at the path-verified evidence level stated in
8435: \path{a7}.  The mathematical content of the article remains carried by the
8436: printed definitions, propositions, lemmas, theorems, and proofs, and external
8437: records carry only the finite evidence claims that cite them.  The integrated
8438: inventory gives the submitted finite obligation surface against which those
8439: theorem-like claims are enumerated; it indexes the obligations and does not prove
8440: them.  This is exactly the bounded claim stated in the theorem.
8441: \end{proof}
8442: 
8443: \begin{proposition}[Post-inventory support-tuple extraction certificate]
8444: \label{prop:post-inventory-support-tuple-extraction-certificate}
8445: For the current source package, the theorem-ledger support tuple is accepted only
8446: in the post-inventory form consisting of
8447: \path{theorem_inventory.json}, \path{theorem_inventory.md},
8448: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log},
8449: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json}, and
8450: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  In that form the extraction log must be generated after the two inventory files
8451: exist, the pass-record ledger must contain the theorem-ledger certificate entry,
8452: and the digest manifest must be regenerated after those three records are in
8453: place.  A digest manifest whose inventory rows predate any member of this tuple
8454: does not certify the current theorem-ledger support coordinate; it certifies only
8455: the earlier local byte state named by its rows.
8456: \end{proposition}
8457: 
8458: \begin{proof}
8459: The tuple has five finite coordinates.  The first two are the regenerated manual
8460: inventory files.  The third is the syntactic extractor output: by
8461: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}, the
8462: extractor reads \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json} and records
8463: only labelled theorem-like environment coverage.  The fourth is the finite
8464: certificate ledger entry for the theorem-obligation surface described in
8465: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  The fifth is the digest
8466: manifest used by Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} to identify the
8467: bytes available under exact supplemental inclusion.
8468: 
8469: If the five coordinates are produced in the stated order, every support claim
8470: about the regenerated theorem ledger is a function of byte strings already fixed
8471: before the digest pass: the inventory bytes are read by the extractor, the
8472: theorem-ledger certificate entry records the finite support route, and the digest
8473: manifest hashes the resulting files and log.  The support tuple is therefore a
8474: finite coordinate of the package.  If, however, the digest rows for
8475: \path{theorem_inventory.json} or \path{theorem_inventory.md} are older than the
8476: accepted inventory, or if the extractor or pass-record ledger is written after
8477: the digest manifest, then one of the bytes used by the theorem-ledger claim is
8478: not among the bytes hashed by the manifest.  By
8479: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, the stronger current
8480: support claim cannot be inferred from a finite record that omits that coordinate.
8481: Thus the old digest remains evidence only for the earlier local byte state and
8482: not for the post-inventory support tuple stated here.
8483: \end{proof}
8484: 
8485: \begin{proposition}[Submitted theorem-inventory availability]
8486: \label{prop:submitted-theorem-inventory-availability}
8487: If the external support-surface condition of
8488: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} is satisfied, then
8489: \path{theorem_inventory.json} is an available submitted certificate coordinate
8490: for the bounded theorem-obligation ledger claim of
8491: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.  If the exact bundle is not
8492: uploaded with matching final digests, the theorem inventory has only local
8493: diagnostic force and cannot support a submitted theorem-ledger claim in the
8494: current package.
8495: \end{proposition}
8496: 
8497: \begin{proof}
8498: The first sentence is the exact-bundle branch of
8499: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} applied to the manifest-exposed
8500: coordinate \path{theorem_inventory.json} and the ledger-exposed coordinate
8501: \path{a7}.  In that branch, the file named by the manifest is available to the
8502: reviewer as part of the submitted finite support surface, so it may be used for
8503: the bounded theorem-ledger claim.  If the exact bundle is not supplied with
8504: matching final digests, Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}
8505: prevents all bundle-backed support claims from being externally instantiated for
8506: this package.  The theorem inventory then remains a local file rather than a
8507: submitted support coordinate, so it cannot carry the submitted theorem-ledger
8508: claim.
8509: \end{proof}
